<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Sirohi State</h1><p class="st-meta">1405 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Sirohi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Deora Chauhan Rajput state of south-western Rajasthan, which held Mount Abu and its Dilwara Jain temples and was famed for its swords.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Deora branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a>, descended from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Chahamanas of Jalor</a>, ruled the hills around Abu in the 14th century. In the early 15th century they established their seat at Sirohi, which became the capital of the state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sirohi was a rugged state on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jodhpur-state">Marwar</a>, often at war with Marwar. Its territory included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a>, with the marble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilwara-temples">Dilwara Jain temples</a>, and Sirohi blades were renowned across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The state signed a treaty with the British in 1823, and in 1845 leased part of Mount Abu for a sanatorium, which became headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sirohi joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in 1949, though Abu was briefly given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> before returning to Rajasthan in 1956. It is now a district of Rajasthan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sirohi-state.jpg" alt="Sirohi State" width="320" height="178"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sirohi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1405 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sirohi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marwari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sirohi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1677&amp;focus=sirohi-state" aria-label="Sirohi State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6614 1262 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sirohi State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6934 1518L7364 1601M6934 1518L7364 1602M6934 1518L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6934 1518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6934 1518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sirohi. <a href="/atlas?year=1677&amp;focus=sirohi-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
